course: dictionary
lesson: 11374
title: Abolition: officially ending something
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Abolition is a noun. It means officially ending something, such as a law, a system or a custom, so that it no longer exists. Look at the words around it to find the clue. «The abolition of the tax made shops cheaper.» Shops became cheaper because the tax was ended, so abolition means ending the tax.
explain: In history, abolition often means ending something unfair. «Many people campaigned for the abolition of child labour, so children could go to school instead of working.» The campaigners wanted to end child labour by law. Always ask: what was ended? The answer is usually the words after «of».
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The abolition movement grew stronger each year.» A movement is a group of people working together for a change, so the abolition movement is the group of people who want something officially ended.
fact: Abolition is a noun: it names the act of ending something.
fact: «Abolition» means the official ending of a law, system or custom.
fact: In history, «abolition» especially means the ending of slavery and the slave trade.
fact: You can say «the abolition of» something, such as «the abolition of a tax».
fact: A campaign for abolition is a long effort by people who want something officially ended.
exercise: «The abolition of the tax made shops cheaper.» What does abolition mean here? | Painting the shops | *Officially ending the tax | A list of shop prices | Raising the tax
exercise: «Many people campaigned for the abolition of child labour.» What is the campaign trying to do? | Open more factories | *End child labour officially | Move children to new schools | Give children more jobs
exercise: What does «abolition» mean? | A small building for workers | *The official ending of a law, system or custom | A new law that starts something | A loud argument
test: «The abolition of slavery was a major step in history.» In this sentence, abolition means what? | The start of a new war | *The official ending of slavery | A school trip to a museum
test: Tom thinks abolition means a big party. What is the correct meaning? | *Officially ending a law, system or custom | A type of tax | A very small room | A big party
exam: «Campaigners worked for years for the abolition of an unfair custom.» What does abolition mean? | Forgetting about the custom | Writing a new custom | *Officially ending the custom | Shouting in the street
